Report where ctor evaluation stopped.

`evalCtors` stops at the first ctor it cannot eval, and says why only on
stdout. An embedder that sets that ctor aside and tries again -- the only way
to eval the ctors after it -- has to find it without the log, and the natural
guess, the first ctor still exported, is right only when evalling started at
all. When the module cannot be instantiated -- its start function calls an
import, say -- no ctor was tried, every retry fails the same way, and the guess
blames one innocent ctor per retry.

So record it: `stopped` is whether evalling stopped before evalling every ctor,
and `stoppedAt` is the ctor it stopped at, or empty when it stopped before
trying any. The catches around the loop are outside `evalCtor()`, and what they
catch can come after instantiation -- serialising a global in `applyToModule()`
throws the same exception -- so the loop keeps the ctor being tried for them to
name.

Nothing is evalled differently, and nothing is printed differently.

